<ArticleLayout
  slug="pro-se-divorce-florida"
  title="Filing for Divorce in Florida Without a Lawyer"
  kicker="Self-Represented"
  lede="Florida has two pro se routes, and which one applies to you turns almost entirely on whether there are minor children. Here is what each requires, what it costs, and where a mediator is worth paying for."
  description="How to file for divorce in Florida without a lawyer: the six-month residency rule, simplified dissolution eligibility, the regular uncontested route, real costs, and when you do not need a mediator at all."
>
  <h2>First, the requirement nobody can waive</h2>
  <p>
    One of you must have <strong>resided in Florida for six months</strong>
    before the petition is filed. That comes from
    <strong>section 61.021 of the Florida Statutes</strong>, it is
    jurisdictional, and it is the single most common reason a self-represented
    filing is rejected. Confirm it before you spend anything else.
  </p>

  <h2>Route one: simplified dissolution of marriage</h2>
  <p>
    The short path. You have to satisfy <strong>all</strong> of the following,
    not most of them:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>No minor or dependent children of the marriage, and the wife is not pregnant</li>
    <li>Neither spouse is seeking alimony</li>
    <li>You agree completely on how property and debts are divided</li>
    <li>Both of you agree the marriage is irretrievably broken</li>
    <li>Both of you are willing to appear at the final hearing</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where that fits, it is <strong>roughly $500 to $800 all in</strong> and
    finalizes in about <strong>30 days</strong>. Florida imposes no mandatory
    waiting period of the kind some states have, which is why the timeline is
    that short.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your county clerk sells the packet, often for around $14, and many clerks
    run a self-help center that will check your forms for completeness. They
    cannot give you legal advice, but catching a defective form before you file
    is most of what a self-represented filer actually needs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>If you have minor children you are not eligible.</strong> There is
    no workaround and no way to structure around it.
  </p>

  <h2>Route two: regular uncontested dissolution</h2>
  <p>
    Where you go if there are children, or if alimony is in play. More
    paperwork, still entirely doable without a lawyer:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Petition for Dissolution of Marriage</li>
    <li>A Family Law Financial Affidavit from each party</li>
    <li>A <strong>Parenting Plan</strong>, mandatory where there are minor children</li>
    <li>Child Support Guidelines Worksheet</li>
    <li>Notice of Social Security Number</li>
    <li>The state-required parenting course, completed before finalizing</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Book the parenting course early. Discovering it is the thing holding up
    your final hearing is a common and entirely avoidable delay.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Parenting Plan is the part people underestimate. It has to specify the
    time-sharing schedule, holidays and school breaks, decision-making
    authority by category, exchange logistics, and how the two of you will
    communicate. Where child support is involved, the
    <a class="link-underline" href="/process/child-support-rules">statutory
    guidelines</a> decide the number, and the 2023 changes altered how
    overnights factor into it.
  </p>

  <h2>Write it specifically, not reasonably</h2>
  <p>
    The most common reason a Parenting Plan fails is vagueness. "Reasonable
    notice," "as agreed," "mutually convenient" — each of those is a future
    argument with a court date attached. <strong>"By text, 48 hours ahead"</strong>
    is not. Over-specify. You can always be more flexible in practice than the
    document requires; you cannot get specificity back once you need it.
  </p>

  <h2>What it actually costs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Petition for dissolution: <strong>about $408</strong></li>
    <li>Issuance of summons: <strong>$10</strong></li>
    <li>Clerk's forms packet: often around $14</li>
    <li>Simplified dissolution, all in: <strong>$500 to $800</strong></li>
    <li>Fully pro se and uncontested with no mediator: <strong>under $500</strong></li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you cannot afford the filing fee, file an <strong>Application for
    Determination of Civil Indigent Status</strong>. Approval waives the
    <em>filing fee</em>. It does <strong>not</strong> cover service of process,
    and it does <strong>not</strong> cover mediation fees. People are caught by
    that distinction constantly, so budget for those separately.
  </p>

  <h2>Where a mediator is worth paying for — and where one is not</h2>
  <p>
    Said plainly, by a mediator: <strong>if you already agree on everything,
    you may not need one.</strong> Buy the packet and file.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mediation earns its fee in one specific situation — you agree on most of
    it and are genuinely stuck on the rest. A couple of hours to unstick a
    parenting schedule is excellent value against the five figures a contested
    case costs. If you are at zero agreement and one of you will not engage,
    mediation will not fix that either.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worth checking before you hire anyone privately, including this practice:
    <strong>court-connected mediation</strong> costs $60 to $120 per person per
    session where combined household income is under $100,000, and is free for
    parties determined indigent. Call your circuit's ADR office and ask. The
    <a class="link-underline" href="/areas-served">areas served</a> page covers
    what several South Florida circuits publish about their own programs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mediating <em>before</em> anyone files is its own approach, and it keeps
    you off the court's clock entirely — see
    <a class="link-underline" href="/process/pre-litigation-disputes">pre-litigation
    disputes</a> for what a signed agreement means when no case exists.
  </p>

  <h2>If you want the paperwork handled</h2>
  <p>
    Two document services exist for people taking the pro se route who would
    rather not assemble the forms themselves. Both are document preparation
    only, and neither is legal advice:
    <a class="link-underline" href="/services/parenting-plan">Parenting Plan
    Preparation</a> prepares only the Parenting Plan, and the
    <a class="link-underline" href="/services/court-packet">Turn-Key Court
    Packet</a> assembles the Plan together with the required Florida Supreme
    Court family-law forms, formatting, and notarization where applicable.
  </p>

  <h2>The one thing worth paying for regardless</h2>
  <p>
    A single attorney review of your agreement before you sign it. Not a
    retainer — one consultation. Property division is effectively permanent
    once a judgment enters, and this is the cheapest insurance available
    anywhere in the process.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
